<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/intermediate/plant-biology">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Plants are <strong>stationary chemists</strong>: rooted in place, they solve every problem
    animals solve with movement — finding food, water, light, mates and defence — using growth
    and chemistry instead. You already know the headline reaction: photosynthesis builds sugar
    from CO₂ and water using light. This lesson walks the whole machine — the leaf where the
    chemistry happens, the roots and vascular plumbing that feed it, and the hormone system that
    lets a fixed organism steer itself through a changing world. Understanding plants is
    understanding how a fixed organism wins, and since plants are the foundation of nearly every
    food chain, it is also understanding where your own meals come from.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the plant body, system by system" widget="equationStep" config={plantCfg} />

  <h2>Photosynthesis: the engine</h2>
  <p>
    The overall equation is 6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ (in light, with chlorophyll). That
    single line hides two linked stages. In the <strong>light-dependent stage</strong>, chlorophyll
    in the thylakoid membranes absorbs light and uses its energy to split water — liberating the
    oxygen (every O₂ molecule you breathe came from a water molecule split by a plant, alga or
    cyanobacterium) and charging the energy carriers ATP and NADPH. In the
    <strong>Calvin cycle</strong>, those carriers power the fixation of CO₂ onto a five-carbon
    sugar, and after a cycle of reductions the six-carbon glucose emerges. No light is needed for
    the Calvin cycle itself — but it cannot run for long without the carriers, which is why
    photosynthesis in the dark quickly stops. Chlorophyll absorbs red and blue light strongly and
    reflects green, which is why leaves look green: that colour is literally the light the plant
    declined to use.
  </p>
  <p>
    The reaction is <strong>limiting-factor controlled</strong>, which makes it a favourite exam
    and greenhouse-management topic. Whichever of light intensity, CO₂ concentration, or
    temperature is scarcest sets the rate; increasing the others does nothing until that one is
    relieved. In practice, on a bright warm day ambient CO₂ (about 0.04% of air) is the ceiling —
    which is why commercial glasshouses enrich the air with CO₂ to roughly 0.1% and gain
    double-digit yield increases.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="photosynthesis saturates with light — and a CO₂ shortage caps the ceiling" widget="plotter" config={photoCfg} />

  <p>
    Read the graph the way a grower would. The green curve saturates: beyond a light intensity of
    about 80 units the rate plateaus near 25, because something else — CO₂ supply, temperature —
    has become the bottleneck. The blue curve shows the same plant under CO₂ limitation: the whole
    curve is squashed, and no amount of extra light pushes it past 15. Worked example: a
    greenhouse at midday in June has abundant light, so light is <em>not</em> the limiting factor;
    if the glasshouse is sealed, CO₂ depletion is the cap, and venting or CO₂ injection raises the
    yield instantly. At dawn or dusk, light is the cap. Temperature compounds this: between about
    0 and 30 °C photosynthesis roughly follows enzyme kinetics (doubling per 10 °C), until
    denaturation pulls it down again past about 40 °C. Diagnosis of a slow crop is therefore a
    chain of substitutions — test each factor in turn — and the answer changes through the day.
  </p>

  <h2>Leaf structure: engineered for exchange</h2>
  <p>
    Every layer of a leaf is an answer to a transport problem. The <strong>waxy cuticle</strong>
    and <strong>upper epidermis</strong> are transparent armour — protective but see-through.
    The <strong>palisade mesophyll</strong> beneath holds the densest chloroplast packing in the
    plant, positioned where light lands first. Below it, the <strong>spongy mesophyll</strong>
    is riddled with air spaces so CO₂ can diffuse freely to every photosynthesising cell — gas
    exchange needs surface area the way the lungs need alveoli. The <strong>lower
    epidermis</strong> is studded with <strong>stomata</strong>, pores flanked by two
    <strong>guard cells</strong> that swell (opening the pore) when the plant has water to spare
    and slack (closing it) under drought stress. And the <strong>vascular bundle</strong> — vein
    — threads through the mesophyll, delivering water within diffusion distance of every cell and
    exporting the sugar made. The design logic is one continuous trade-off: CO₂ can only enter
    through open pores, but open pores leak water vapour. A leaf is a factory whose air intake
    vents are also its main leak — which is why desert plants open stomata only at night and
    why wilting closes them as an emergency measure.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "plants get their mass from the soil." Van Helmont showed in
    the 1640s that a willow grown for five years in 90 kg of soil gained 74 kg while the soil
    barely changed. The truth is more extreme than even his conclusion: <strong>about 95% of a
    tree's dry mass is carbon pulled from the air as CO₂</strong>, plus water from the soil.
    Forests are solidified atmosphere.</p>

  <h2>Roots: the hidden half</h2>
  <p>
    Roots do four jobs: anchor, absorb water, absorb mineral ions, and store reserves. Absorption
    is concentrated behind the growing tip in thousands of <strong>root hairs</strong> —
    extensions of epidermal cells that multiply the absorbing surface by several-fold (a single
    rye plant was measured with billions of root hairs adding thousands of square metres
    of surface). Uptake is
    largely <strong>active transport</strong>: ions like nitrate and potassium are moved in
    against their gradients using ATP, which is why roots are metabolically expensive tissue.
    Water follows passively by osmosis, drawn in by the ion-rich root cells. Most plants also
    run a partnership with fungi: <strong>mycorrhizae</strong> wrap or penetrate the roots and
    extend the effective absorptive network up to ten-fold, trading phosphorus and other scarce
    minerals for plant sugar. Roughly 90% of plant species have them, and the oldest fossil
    plants show them — the partnership is as old as land plants themselves.
  </p>

  <h2>The vascular system: plumbing without pumps</h2>
  <p>
    Two tissues run through root, stem and leaf. <strong>Xylem</strong> — dead, hollow, lignified
    tubes — carries water and minerals <em>upward</em> from root to leaf. Its engine is not in
    the plant at all: the <strong>cohesion–tension theory</strong> holds that water evaporating
    from leaf mesophyll (transpiration) pulls on the water column below, and hydrogen bonding
    makes water cohesive enough to behave like a rope under tension — a continuous chain from
    leaf to root, hauled up by evaporation at the top. A large tree can transport hundreds of
    litres a day this way, with zero moving parts, to heights of over 100 metres. The physics
    fails if the chain breaks (an air embolism), so drought that severs the water columns can
    kill a tree even without freezing or fire.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Phloem</strong> — living cells joined end to end through sieve plates — carries sugar
    and other organics <em>wherever they are needed</em>: leaves to roots in summer, storage
    organs to growing buds in spring. Flow is driven by <strong>mass flow</strong>: loading sugar
    at the source draws water in by osmosis, raising pressure; unloading at the sink releases it.
    Sap literally flows from high to low pressure. The direction is reversible, which xylem never
    is — and the system's vulnerability is illustrated by <strong>aphids</strong>, which tap
    phloem with needle mouthparts and drink pressurised sugar solution directly. Ring-barking a
    tree (cutting the phloem in the bark all round) starves the roots while leaving the water
    supply untouched — the tree dies from the bottom up, not the top down.
  </p>
  <p>
    The two systems explain the classic deficiency symptoms gardeners diagnose. Sugars made in the
    leaf need nitrogen to become amino acids and proteins, so a nitrate-starved plant is stunted
    and pale no matter how brightly it photosynthesises. Magnesium sits at the centre of every
    chlorophyll molecule — the mineral is literally the pivot of the light-capturing machinery —
    so magnesium deficiency shows as yellowing between the veins of old leaves, which the plant
    cannibalises first. Iron is needed for chlorophyll synthesis too, but since it cannot be
    mobilised from old leaves, its deficiency yellows the <em>youngest</em> leaves first: the
    pattern of which leaves yellow is a diagnostic of which element is missing. Sugar supply,
    mineral supply and the water that carries them — the whole vascular story — shows up in the
    colour of a leaf.
  </p>

  <h2>Plant hormones: steering without nerves</h2>
  <p>
    With no nervous system, plants coordinate through chemical signals, and each hormone is a
    word in that language. <strong>Auxin</strong>, made in shoot tips, makes cells elongate; when
    light strikes one side, auxin migrates to the shaded side, which then grows faster — the tip
    bends toward the light (<strong>phototropism</strong>). Roots show <strong>gravitropism</strong>
    with the same chemical: auxin accumulates on the lower side, where it <em>inhibits</em> root
    elongation, so roots bend down. <strong>Gibberellins</strong> trigger germination (awakening
    the embryo and mobilising starch reserves in the seed) and drive stem elongation — the dwarf
    wheat varieties of the Green Revolution carried mutations in gibberellin genes.
    <strong>Cytokinins</strong> promote cell division and delay leaf ageing; <strong>abscisic
    acid</strong> is the stress hormone, closing stomata in drought and enforcing seed dormancy;
    <strong>ethylene</strong>, a gas, coordinates ripening and leaf fall — and because it is
    airborne, one over-ripening fruit ethylene-bombs its neighbours, the chemistry behind "one
    bad apple spoils the barrel" and behind the trick of ripening green bananas in a sealed bag.
  </p>
  <p>
    Hormone chemistry is also agriculture and warfare at once. Synthetic auxins are selective
    weedkillers (2,4-D distorts broadleaf weeds but grasses barely respond). Rooting powders are
    auxin. Seedless grapes and modern dwarf rice varieties are gibberellin manipulation. And the
    plant pharmacy is broader still: <strong>caffeine, nicotine, tannins and latex</strong> are
    defence chemicals — poisons, glues and alarms — and many human medicines (aspirin, morphine,
    quinine, taxol) are plant defence chemistry borrowed. Plants also signal to each other:
    damaged acacias release airborne ethylene-like signals that neighbours respond to by
    tannin-loading their leaves, and mycorrhizal networks shuttle nutrients and signals between
    trees ("the wood wide web" — real, though early popular claims were oversold).
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Quantify the water economy and the trade-offs sharpen. A maize plant transpires roughly 200
    litres over a growing season; a mature oak can lose several hundred litres on a hot day. In
    terms of raw materials, that water is almost entirely a by-product: only about 1% of the
    water a plant takes up ends up in its tissues — the rest is the unavoidable price of keeping
    stomata open for CO₂. Plants run photosynthetic variants that tighten the pricing: C4 plants
    (maize, sugarcane) pre-concentrate CO₂ in specialised cells, paying extra ATP to nearly
    eliminate wasteful photorespiration; CAM plants (cacti, pineapples) open stomata only at
    night and store CO₂ as organic acids, trading a day's growth against a desert's drought. In
    hot climates the C4 advantage is large enough that maize out-yields wheat per unit water.
  </p>
  <p>
    The transport physics has measurable limits. Sap in xylem is under negative pressure —
    tension — of up to roughly −2 to −4 MPa at the top of tall trees, approaching the tensile
    limit of water itself; cavitation (bubble formation) under drought is a live research topic
    in forest ecology and a key reason drought-driven tree mortality is rising with climate
    change. Phloem flow rates are surprisingly respectable too — around 1 metre per hour in
    trees, driven by pressure differences of only a few atmospheres. And hormone signalling is
    now known to interlock with electrical signalling: plants propagate slow electrical impulses
    (via ion channels, not nerves) after wounding, coordinating hormone release across the plant
    in minutes. A fixed organism, it turns out, still runs a nervous system of sorts — it just
    speaks in salts, sugars and gases.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Taiz &amp; Zeiger, Plant Physiology; OpenStax, Biology 2e (Plant Structure and Function); Wohlleben, The Hidden Life of Trees (popular, with caveats); Cambridge IGCSE Biology 0610 syllabus, unit 6.</p>
